When the data the user asks for is in two databases in Metabase, build one query for each database and run each one with `run_query` at its largest `row_limit`, with the aggregation and filters in each query. Then combine the rows in your answer, and say that you used one query per database. If a result is truncated, don't answer from it, because the rows you can't see can change the answer. Make that query coarser, with an aggregation at a higher level or a filter on the keys the other result returned, and run it again. If it is still truncated, tell the user that the data is too large to combine, and say what you can do instead.
